LayerCanvas: Infographic Generation in AI Learning Assistants via Hybrid Layer-Set Diffusion Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.18763v1 [cs.CV] 13 Oct 2026 LayerCanvas: Infographic Generation in AI Learning Assistants via Hybrid Layer-Set Diffusion Keshav Gupta † † thanks: Work done during an internship at LinkedIn Inc. Affiliation: University of California, San Diego Jeremy Owen Affiliation: LinkedIn Inc. Christopher Lloyd Affiliation: LinkedIn Inc. Shaunak Mishra Affiliation: LinkedIn Inc. Manav Sharma Affiliation: LinkedIn Inc. Abstract Online learning platforms are increasingly deploying AI assistants to provide personalized, on-demand tutoring. These can leverage infographics to communicate complex information and improve the learning experience. However, automating effective infographic generation remains a challenging task, requiring a combination of visual richness with precise typography, structured layouts, and continued editability. Existing representation methods satisfy these goals only in part: Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) representations preserve geometry, hierarchy, and text but provide limited aesthetic flexibility, while raster diffusion models produce richer appearances by flattening content and layout into a single image. Conditioning a diffusion model on a rasterized SVG does not resolve this conflict because the raster preserves approximate placement but discards element ownership, occlusion relationships, and the structure needed to modify and recompose the result. We introduce LayerCanvas , a hybrid approach that retains SVG as the semantic, structural, and typographic program while using diffusion to aestheticize non-text content. Rather than generating one flattened raster, LayerCanvas partitions SVG elements into z-ordered sets of mutually non-overlapping elements and processes them through communicating parallel RGB generation streams. The resulting layers are independently editable and deterministically recomposed, while text is rendered from preserved vector metadata. Our approach combines rich visual synthesis with accurate typography and localized editing. LayerCanvas substantially improves text fidelity over state-of-the-art raster image generators while achieving the highest overall aesthetic quality. We further demonstrate non-destructive content editing, localization, asset reuse, and animation. Figure 1: LayerCanvas generates editable, text-faithful infographics. Current SOTA text-to-image models such as Qwen-Image-2512 ( Qwen Team, 2025a ) and Ideogram-4 ( Ideogram AI, 2026 ) flatten the graphic and often garble text (top). Given a vector layout, LayerCanvas generates ordered RGB layers and composes them with exact vector text (middle), enabling localization and illustration edits without changing unrelated content (bottom). 1 Introduction Visual explanations are a fundamental component of modern learning platforms, where instructional content is increasingly presented through a combination of text, diagrams, illustrations, and infographics. Pairing words with pictures improves comprehension and retention relative to text alone ( Mayer, 2009 ; Clark and Paivio, 1991 ) , while infographics can make dense information more accessible and memorable ( Bateman et al., 2010 ; Bhat and Alyahya, 2024 ) . As learning platforms increasingly deliver and adapt instructional materials digitally, there is a growing need to automatically generate visual explanations that are both pedagogically effective and easy to revise as content evolves. Such explanations must communicate through precise labels and clear visual hierarchy while also providing the visual richness expected from modern image generators ( Rombach et al., 2022 ; Podell et al., 2024 ; Chen et al., 2025 ) . Meeting these requirements is challenging: precision, structure, and editability are naturally supported by representations such as Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) ( W3C SVG Working Group, 2011 ) , whereas visual richness is most readily produced by raster generative models. This representational contrast has a fundamental tradeoff. Vector graphics make geometry, grouping, text, and rendering order explicit, which makes direct manipulation possible ( W3C SVG Working Group, 2011 ) , but automatically generated SVGs ( Rodriguez et al., 2025 ; Wu et al., 2023 ; Xing et al., 2024 ; Wu et al., 2024 ) often appear visually rigid, and stylizing them by hand is costly and difficult to scale. On the other hand, diffusion and flow models provide richer aesthetic priors ( Rombach et al., 2022 ; Saharia et al., 2022 ; Esser et al., 2024 ) , but synthesizing the artifact as a single raster makes exact typography and local intervention difficult; even state-of-the-art image models render dense, legible text unreliably, motivating specialized text-rendering methods ( Liu et al., 2023b ; Chen et al., 2023 ; Tuo et al., 2024 ) . These limitations are especially consequential for learning platforms, which must serve learners worldwide by localizing explanations into different languages and adapting layouts across display formats, from desktop interfaces to mobile screens. Supporting these uses requires text to remain disentangled from imagery and layout components to remain independently adjustable, properties that are lost when the infographic is flattened into a raster. A natural way then is to rasterize the SVG layout and use it as a conditioning signal for a diffusion model, letting the vector structure guide where content goes while diffusion supplies appearance. Simply rasterizing an SVG for conditioning does not resolve the mismatch: it preserves coarse spatial placement but discards element ownership, occlusion relationships, and the structure needed to recompose the graphic after an edit. This loss of structure cannot be addressed simply by strengthening raster conditioning. A system designed for editable generation should preserve these compositional units throughout synthesis instead of attempting to recover their ownership from pixels afterward. Motivated by this principle, LayerCanvas combines the structure of SVGs with the visual capabilities of diffusion models. The SVG defines the semantic layout, hierarchy, paint order, and typography. Its non-text elements are partitioned into ordered, non-overlapping layers and generated through communicating parallel RGB streams, which are then alpha-composited according to the SVG z-order. Text nodes are kept outside the diffusion process entirely and rendered directly from their preserved vector metadata (Figure 1 ). Outputs remain editable, so users can replace text, move or hide groups, change the z-order, reuse illustrations, or specify animation trajectories. This makes the representation well suited to visual authoring workflows where generated content needs to remain editable and reusable, including educational content creation. Training such a model requires examples that pair SVG with the corresponding rendered layers and final image. To create this paired supervision, we design a custom multi-agent framework, following recent work on agentic pipelines ( Jain et al., 2026 ; Huang et al., 2026 ) . The agents create concepts and SVG layouts, add illustrations, produce text-free targets and masks, and check that each example renders and reconstructs correctly. Our contributions are: • A hybrid formulation that separates deterministic SVG structure and typography from diffusion-based aestheticization; • A multi-agent pipeline that produces and validates paired SVGs, text-free targets, layers, masks, and provenance metadata; • A rendering-aligned layer representation that supports parallel generation, explicit occlusion modeling, and deterministic z-order composition, yielding over 25 % 25\% higher placement-exact OCR character accuracy and approximately 27 % 27\% higher aggregate aesthetic quality than the strongest raster baseline • Practical workflows for non-destructive editing, localization, asset reuse, and animated visual explanations. 2 Related Work Visual documents and typography. Automatic graphic design combines templates, constraints, retrieval, and learned layouts; neural methods generate sets or sequences of labeled regions, including alignment and overlap structure ( Li et al., 2019 ; Gupta et al., 2021 ; Inoue et al., 2023 ) . Such representations organize posters, slides, and documents, but do not by themselves resolve illustration appearance, occlusion, or editable object ownership. Programmatic graphics preserve structure, yet hand-authored styling and rigid templates scale poorly to open-ended concepts. Image generators provide richer aesthetics, and text-aware methods introduce character encoders, rendered-glyph conditions, OCR objectives, or localized controls ( Chen et al., 2023 ; Yang et al., 2024b ; Tuo et al., 2024 ) . Instead, we retain SVG as the source of truth for geometry and typography, remove text from diffusion targets, and render editable vector text after aestheticization. Controllable, object-centric, and layered generation. ControlNet and T2I-Adapter condition pretrained diffusion models on spatial signals ( Zhang et al., 2023 ; Mou et al., 2024 ) , while grounded, reference-conditioned, and regional methods add box, entity, exemplar, or prompt-region controls ( Li et al., 2023 ; Ye et al., 2023 ; Bar-Tal et al., 2023 ) . These approaches improve spatial control but typically produce a single entangled raster or latent field, limiting direct editing after sampling. Object-centric, compositional, and layer-aware models separate entities, concepts, transparency, or foreground/background content ( Burgess et al., 2019 ; Locatello et al., 2020 ; Avrahami et al., 2023 ; Zhang and Agrawala, 2024 ) , with recent methods explicitly generating or recovering editable image layers ( Yang et al., 2024a ; Yin et al., 2025 ; Wang et al., 2026 ) . In contrast, LayerCanvas uses SVG layer identity as explicit conditioning, packing mutually non-overlapping elements into ordered RGB layer sets. This known layer structure makes output decomposition and local intervention intrinsic to the representation, rather than requiring layers to be recovered post hoc. Agentic multimodal curation and differentiation. Foundation models have increasingly been used to generate instructions, propose examples, and curate synthetic multimodal supervision ( Wang et al., 2023 ; Liu et al., 2023a ; Gadre et al., 2023 ) . More recent agentic pipelines extend this paradigm by coordinating specialized agents for scientific tasks, including translating NeRF papers into executable code and automating the generation of editable scientific figures ( Jain et al., 2026 ; Huang et al., 2026 ) . These systems demonstrate the potential of agentic workflows for generating and iteratively refining structured scientific artifacts. Our pipeline builds on this agentic paradigm by combining specialized generative and validation agents with deterministic SVG parsing, rendering, OCR, overlap analysis, and alpha-composition checks, producing mechanically validated training records. 3 Problem Setup and Method 3.1 Overview Infographics combine two capabilities that are usually separated. SVGs provide exact layout and typography, whereas diffusion models provide rich raster appearance but collapse the scene into one pixel grid ( Podell et al., 2024 ; Rombach et al., 2022 ) . Conditioning diffusion on a rasterized SVG preserves coarse geometry but loses element boundaries and information about which elements cover others. LayerCanvas instead keeps the SVG as the main scene description and generates its non-text content as an ordered set of editable raster layers. Figure 2 summarizes the approach. A renderer creates training layers from the SVG, elements that do not overlap are packed into ordered groups, and all group