rations but garble or invent text and drift from the prescribed layout whereas LayerCanvas keeps the layer layout and composites exact, legible vector text. 4 Experiments 4.1 Setup Dataset - We develop InfoLayers, a corpus of 23,960 paired infographic records on a 768 × 512 768	imes 512 canvas with a multi-agent pipeline. Each sample pairs a structural SVG with a text-free and a composited target, together with the per-layer supervision our model consumes: amodal and visible masks, paint order, and a hierarchy/overlap graph. Construction and layout statistics are given in Appendix A . Baselines - We compare four methods on shared held-out layouts. The LLM-authored SVG reference recolors and completes the structural SVG and renders it, giving exact vector text by construction but no learned aestheticization. The two prompt-only open-weight SOTA diffusion baselines, Qwen-Image-2512 ( Qwen Team, 2025a ) and Ideogram-4 ( Ideogram AI, 2026 ) , are each conditioned on the design-concept prompt derived from the input SVG layout; for Ideogram-4 this prompt is constructed from the same SVG layout, with its structural elements serialized into the structured prompt format the model expects. LayerCanvas is our grouped layer-set diffusion model with deterministic exact-SVG text compositing with the MMDiT backbone as Ideogram AI (2026) . For evaluation, all methods are placed on a 768 × 512 768{	imes}512 canvas. Metrics - Text fidelity is scored two ways. Placement-exact fidelity uses ground-truth-region OCR Tesseract OCR contributors (2026) at 768 × 512 768{	imes}512 , and reports character accuracy and exact-match rate. Placement-agnostic fidelity runs full-image OCR at the same resolution and measures word recall, how much of the ground-truth text appears anywhere in the image, so a baseline that renders readable text at the wrong position still gets credit. The two exact-text methods place text from the SVG by construction, so their remaining gap from 1.0 1.0 is OCR error rather than real text mistakes. For visual quality we use a VLM as a judge : Claude Opus 4.8 ( Anthropic, 2026 ) scores each design from 0 0 to 5 5 on illustration appeal, typography, and semantic coverage, and we report Avg , the mean of the three. The full evaluation protocol is given in Appendix B.2 . 4.2 Main comparison Table 1 reports the comparison on the test set (construction in Appendix B ). LayerCanvas combines strong visual appeal with text fidelity, outperforming both raster baselines across all text metrics while approaching the exact-text SVG reference. Ideogram-4 Ideogram AI (2026) generates aesthetic looking samples, but it still trails LayerCanvas on text fidelity; Qwen-Image-2512 Qwen Team (2025a) performs poorly on both text placement and recovery. Under the primary Claude judge, LayerCanvas is the strongest learned method across illustration appeal, typography, semantic coverage, and aggregate quality, and slightly exceeds the SVG reference overall. The same learned-method ranking holds under independent Gemini 3.7 Flash ( Google DeepMind, 2026 ) and GPT-5.6 Sol ( OpenAI, 2026 ) judges (Appendix Table 5 ). Figure 3 shows the same appeal-text trade-off qualitatively. Additional results are in Appendix B.3 . Inference compute details for LayerCanvas are in Appendix B.4.2 . 4.3 Representation ablation We compare grouped LayerCanvas with a single-raster conditioning baseline to test whether explicit layer separation improves editability. The baseline conditions the same MMDiT model ( Ideogram AI, 2026 ) directly on the rasterized SVG using T2I-Adapter ( Mou et al., 2024 ) . A single stream must learn to render the whole graphic at once, which becomes harder as the layout grows denser. In contrast, each LayerCanvas layer contains non-overlapping elements, making it simpler to learn before the layers are composed. Under fixed noise, LayerCanvas better preserves the moved element and surrounding layout, while the control redraws unrelated content (Figure 4 ). It also improves text recall and all VLM scores, raising average quality from 3.55 to 3.77 (Table 2 ). Figure 4: Representation ablation: Each column shows the source SVG, fixed-noise base generation, and the translated version by perturbing the base SVG for LayerCanvas and the single-raster as control. Red arrows show the shift from the base position in the translated cases. LayerCanvas better preserves object identity and unrelated layout content. Table 2: Comparison of Different Conditioning Representations on validation set Method Char Exact Recall Appeal Typog. Cov. Avg Single-raster Conditioning 0.851 0.568 0.783 2.78 3.56 4.32 3.55 LayerCanvas 0.823 0.530 0.795 2.84 3.76 4.72 3.77 5 Downstream applications Because LayerCanvas preserves SVG structure alongside independently addressable raster layers, a generated design can be adapted without regenerating the whole image. Vector text supports changes to wording, typography, and language, while separate visual layers enable animation, asset reuse, and local element edits such as removing a card or swapping an icon while preserving unaffected content. Retained grouping and paint order further support layout retargeting, re-theming, and SVG-level corrections while limiting changes to the intended components. Together, these capabilities turn a generated infographic into a reusable authoring artifact. We highlight three below; the rest are detailed in Appendix C . 5.1 Text editing and localization End-to-end raster generators must repaint text whenever wording, typography, or language changes. LayerCanvas instead generates only the visual substrate and preserves typography as styled SVG nodes, so copy, style, and locale changes require no diffusion pass and cannot alter the generated artwork (Figure 13 ). 5.2 Independent layer animation We adapt the zero-shot animation principle of Text2Video-Zero ( Khachatryan et al., 2023 ) , in which the motion and appearance priors of a pretrained text-to-image diffusion model produce a temporally linked sequence without video-specific training. Whereas a flattened document would expose the complete page to this update, LayerCanvas lets the temporal operation target one named image layer while all other layers remain fixed: every unselected image layer and the full-resolution SVG typography are copied identically into every frame. Figure 5: Independent animation of one LayerCanvas region. A Text2Video-Zero-inspired image-diffusion trajectory produces an open-to-closed blink. Only the selected eye region changes across the five frames; the surrounding illustration, layout, and vector typography remain fixed. 5.3 SVG-space refinement and faithful diffusion propagation An upstream layout proposal may contain a misplaced, mislabeled, or incorrectly styled element. In LayerCanvas, the SVG is a persistent control surface: the element can be corrected directly, and the model propagates that correction to the raster without retraining or redesigning the rest of the document. Figure 6: Closed-loop SVG correction. Each row shows an incorrect SVG and render, the direct SVG edit, and the corrected render. The edited element moves to the requested location while unrelated content remains stable. 6 Conclusion We formulate infographic generation as structured aestheticization: a deterministic SVG supplies geometry, hierarchy, typography, while parallel layer-set generation supplies visual appearance. Aligning this representation with the renderer supports many downstream applications like editing and artifact reuse. Enforcing generalization to more layouts and improving transformations under changed occlusion remain important research directions. References Anthropic (2026) Anthropic Introducing Claude Opus 4.8 . Note: https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-opus-4-8 Product announcement, May 28, 2026 Cited by: §A.6 , §B.2 , Table 5 , §4.1 . Avrahami et al. (2023) O. Avrahami, K. Aberman, O. Fried, D. Cohen-Or, and D. Lischinski Break-a-scene: extract